psule labeling and FDA oral solution labeling support capsule and oral solution/syrup formulations.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/012380s037lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2009/080258s025lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Ethosuximide; Zarontin","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Zarontin","rationale":"Zarontin is the FDA-labeled proprietary/brand name; ethosuximide is already the generic name and should not be repeated as a trade name unless the row intentionally stores generic-labeled product names in this field.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/012380s037lbl.pdf","https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%220e008f33-70a1-4bc6-b3a0-d45214418ab6%22&limit=5"]},{"current_value":"","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"","rationale":"No separate alternate generic name requiring a duplicate-row merge was identified in the trusted sources checked; Zarontin belongs in trade/search aliases, not alternate generic names.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/012380s037lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20200383/"]},{"current_value":"Zarontin","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"Zarontin","rationale":"Zarontin is a valid brand/search alias for ethosuximide.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/012380s037lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA Orange Book products file; FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA Orange Book products file; FDA/openFDA labeling; FDA-approved labeling PDFs; NIH/NCBI Bookshelf; PubMed","rationale":"DailyMed should not be used for the boxed-warning field, and the row needs NIH/NCBI/PubMed sources for pharmacokinetic and enzyme-effect facts not present in FDA labeling.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-approvals-and-databases/orange-book-data-files","https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%220e008f33-70a1-4bc6-b3a0-d45214418ab6%22&limit=5","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544244/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9606477/"]},{"current_value":"N/A - selected FDA label does not define clinically meaningful enzyme induction or inhibition; label notes interactions with other antiepileptic drug serum levels.","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"Neither enzyme-inducing nor enzyme-inhibiting; ethosuximide may increase phenytoin levels, and valproic acid has been reported to both increase and decrease ethosuximide levels.","rationale":"A PubMed review states ethosuximide is neither an inducer nor inhibitor of drug metabolism, while FDA labeling supports the phenytoin/valproate interaction wording.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9606477/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/012380s037lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"N/A - selected FDA/DailyMed label does not provide a half-life value.","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"30 hours in children; 50-60 hours in adults.","rationale":"The selected FDA label may omit half-life, but a trusted NIH/NCBI source provides the half-life range, so N/A is incomplete for the data field.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544244/"]},{"current_value":"N/A - selected FDA/DailyMed label does not identify a major metabolic organ; label advises caution and monitoring with liver or renal disease.","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"Hepatic metabolism; about 80% metabolized to inactive metabolites, primarily by CYP3A4.","rationale":"NIH/NCBI sources identify hepatic metabolism and CYP3A4 involvement; LiverTox also describes liver metabolism via cytochrome P450.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544244/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547870/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12637244/"]},{"current_value":"1500 mg/day; FDA label states doses exceeding 1.5 g/day should be used only under strict physician supervision.","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"1500 mg/day; FDA label states doses exceeding 1.5 g/day should be used only under strict physician supervision.","rationale":"FDA labeling verifies the 1.5 g/day threshold; the deterministic mismatch appears to be a unit/string-matching false positive.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/012380s037lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2009/080258s025lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"250 mg/day age 3-6 years or 500 mg/day age 6 years and older initial labeled dose; titrate to control.","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"250 mg/day age 3-6 years or 500 mg/day age 6 years and older initial labeled dose; titrate to control.","rationale":"FDA dosage labeling supports these initial doses and titration to response.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/012380s037lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Initial: 250 mg/day age 3-6 years or 500 mg/day age 6 years and older; increase by 250 mg every 4-7 days; optimal pediatric dose often 20 mg/kg/day per FDA label.","field":"typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"Initial: 250 mg/day age 3-6 years or 500 mg/day age 6 years and older; increase by 250 mg every 4-7 days; optimal pediatric dose often 20 mg/kg/day per FDA label.","rationale":"FDA labeling supports the starting doses, 250 mg increments every 4-7 days, and 20 mg/kg/day optimal pediatric dose statement.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/012380s037lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2009/080258s025lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA labeling states ethosuximide suppresses the 3-cycle-per-second spike-wave activity of absence seizures and reduces attack frequency apparently by depression of motor cortex and elevation of CNS seizure threshold.","field":"mechanism_of_action","proposed_value":"FDA labeling states ethosuximide suppresses the 3-cycle-per-second spike-wave activity of absence seizures and reduces attack frequency apparently by depression of motor cortex and elevation of CNS seizure threshold.","rationale":"This is an accurate statement of the FDA label mechanism language. A more modern T-type calcium channel description is also supported by NIH/NCBI, but the current cell is specifically framed as FDA-label wording.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/012380s037lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544244/"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA-approved labeling","rationale":"The mechanism statement is supportable from FDA-approved labeling PDFs, so the source should not depend on DailyMed wording.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/012380s037lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA label","field":"mechanism_source_tier","proposed_value":"FDA label","rationale":"The cited mechanism statement is supported by FDA labeling.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/012380s037lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Moderate","field":"mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"Moderate","rationale":"Moderate confidence is defensible because FDA labeling gives older pharmacologic wording and NIH/NCBI describes the T-type calcium channel mechanism as thought/accepted but mechanistically complex.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/012380s037lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544244/"]},{"current_value":"Other / unclear","field":"filter_mechanism","proposed_value":"T-type calcium channel blocker","rationale":"NIH/NCBI describes ethosuximide as disrupting thalamocortical absence seizure circuitry by blocking/reducing T-type calcium currents; the current filter is less accurate.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544244/"]},{"current_value":"Limited/unknown","field":"filter_metabolism","proposed_value":"Hepatic; CYP3A4","rationale":"Trusted NIH/NCBI and PubMed sources identify hepatic/CYP metabolism, so the filter should not remain limited/unknown.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544244/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12637244/"]},{"current_value":"Unknown/limited","field":"filter_enzyme_effect","proposed_value":"None / not clinically meaningful","rationale":"PubMed review evidence supports ethosuximide as neither an inducer nor inhibitor of drug metabolism.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9606477/"]},{"current_value":"No QT interval effect described in selected FDA label.","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"No QT interval effect described in selected FDA label.","rationale":"The FDA labeling reviewed does not describe a QT interval effect; this supports the narrow wording, but it should not be overread as proof from a dedicated QT study.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/012380s037lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No known meaningful QT effect","field":"filter_qt_effect","proposed_value":"No QT effect described in FDA label","rationale":"Absence of QT wording in the FDA label supports label silence, not a positive no-effect finding from a QT study.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"insufficient_evidence","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/012380s037lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"N/A 0%: FDA label lists anorexia, gastric upset, nausea, vomiting, cramps, abdominal pain, weight loss, diarrhea, drowsiness, headache, dizziness, euphoria, hiccups, irritability, hyperactivity, lethargy, fatigue, ataxia, rash/urticaria/SJS, blood dyscrasias, and psychiatric symptoms without quantified incidence percentages.","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"N/A 0%: FDA label lists anorexia, gastric upset, nausea, vomiting, cramps, abdominal pain, weight loss, diarrhea, drowsiness, headache, dizziness, euphoria, hiccups, irritability, hyperactivity, lethargy, fatigue, ataxia, rash/urticaria/SJS, blood dyscrasias, and psychiatric symptoms without quantified incidence percentages.","rationale":"FDA labeling lists these adverse reactions without incidence percentages. Treat the 0% portion as an N/A placeholder rather than a true incidence value.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/012380s037lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2009/080258s025lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Approved Prior to Jan 1, 1982 (FDA Orange Book)","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"Approved prior to Jan 1, 1982 (FDA Orange Book)","rationale":"FDA Orange Book documentation uses this phrase for products approved before 